Notes on data brief
BCN


•	Phase 1 is largely Southern Norway. Phase 2 may involved lower temperatures 
due to more geologically diverse localessuch as Bodø and Tromsø.

•	“Not all household participating in the pricing experiment answered a survey, 
  and those answering survey 1 did not necessarily share their electricity consumption data.” 
  o	Survey 1 may not have consumption data but could have survey responses. 
  This could be interesting to analyze the survey answers depending on work load. 

•	Both winters of the experiment should NOT be considered interchangeable. 
  Lengths of the experiments, number of participant vary. 
  This could be attributed to COVID-19 lock downs which were initiated March 12, 2020 in Norway. 

•	Experiment 1: Feb 11, 2020 -> March 9, 2020. 28 days total.
  Experiment 2: Dec 16, 2020 -> March 25, 2021 101days total.

•	The researchers did record for some time before and after each experiment,
  according to the document:
  Phase 1: 36 days before pricing experiment and 11 days after
  Phase 2: 15 days before pricing experiment and 1 day after. 

•	Phase 1 only covered 7 of the listed price signals: A_5, A_10, B_5, B_10, C, P_5, and P_10. 

•	Surveys varied greatly in content and participants:
  o	Survey 1: Largely Demographic, Housing information, Electrical Equipment. 
  
  o	Survey 2: More focused on reactions and perspectives on the price signs. 
  
  o	Survey 3: Combination of Survey 1 + 2, a little more in depth. 
  
  o	It’s important to note that Survey 3 was also sent out to Households 
    that were not part of the project but lived in the same cities. 

•	While 3 electrical suppliers are listed in the study they are not the sole power
  suppliers in the region’s studied, as mentioned that some members in Phase 2 
  were not able to participate as they were no longer contracted to the electrical 
  supplier they were in Phase 1.
  
  o	There was no stated rationale for choosing the specific suppliers used 
  
  o	Another reason is that data couldn’t be retrieved from the national database Elhub, 
  though again no reason or explanation was given. 

•	On page 18 I think is one of the biggest considerations about phase 1 they write:
  “These household s are not representative of the general population, and the final sample had an overweight of households 
  living in houses, with an age between 50-80 years, men and couples without children.” 
  They attribute this to the low recruitment rate at 3.4% because it was a complicated process to sign up. 
  o	They worked to make the participants more representative in phase 2 as well as bring recruitment rates up to 40-50% 
